# Commit LF to the repository regardless of the developer's OS.
#
# core.autocrlf=true on Windows was silently committing CRLF blobs: that is
# what corrupted the fdroiddata metadata (`fdroid lint` rejects CRLF), and it
# is why the generic rule alone is not enough — `text=auto` lets git apply its
# binary sniff and it had cached a `-text` (binary) flag on an otherwise plain
# Kotlin file. The explicit rules below force text treatment with LF for
# source, resources and the metadata tooling reads, so those files can never
# be committed with CRLF again.
#
# Binary assets (PNG, TTF, WebP, JKS) are unaffected: none of the patterns
# below match them, and the generic rule still leaves them byte-exact.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.kt         text eol=lf
*.kts        text eol=lf
*.java       text eol=lf
*.xml        text eol=lf
*.md         text eol=lf
*.txt        text eol=lf
*.yml        text eol=lf
*.yaml       text eol=lf
*.json       text eol=lf
*.properties text eol=lf
*.pro        text eol=lf
*.gitignore  text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf
